as the end to the cdc moratorium nears a  lot of people facing eviction here in  nevada  the state announced today there's now an  option to keep people in their homes and  help landlords get the money they need  sasha loftis joining us in the newsroom  to break down assemblyville 486  and also some of the options it offers  sasha denise governor sysolak and local  leaders say  their goal here is to keep people in  their homes as the cdc eviction  moratorium ends later this week  they also want to provide landlords with  assistance to pay their bills  assembly bill 486 which was signed into  law earlier this year prevents a tenant  from being evicted  if they have proof they filed a rental  assistance application  the court will then place a hold on the  case and arrangements will be made for  the landlord  until the rental assistance goes through  eligible landlords who own single family  homes can also apply for assistance  through this program  governor sissellac spoke on the  importance of it for everyone  as the pandemic has changed and evolved  so has our approach to the moratorium  and we've been able to build out rental  assistance programs  with state and county partners mediation  programs with the court system  and most recently by partnering with the  nevada legislature  to create a so-called glide path for  evictions  it is important to note in order to  qualify for this you have to have that  application for rental assistance  already in so leaders recommend applying  now before the moratorium  expires now i did ask about support for  those unable to keep up with rising rent  here in nevada  though this particular legislation won't  help with that clark county is working  on a specific support program if you  want to learn more about ab486 or apply  we have information  on our website 8newsnow.com reporting in  the newsroom tonight sasha loftis  8 news now
 

